      h1: 'What a construction supervisor does and what project supervision includes',
      metaTitle: 'What Does a Construction Supervisor Do? Duties, Inspections & Why It Pays | Afik Hanahal',
      description: 'A construction supervisor represents the developer or owner opposite the contractor: checks that construction matches the plans and standards, approves payments, tracks schedule and budget and documents. What supervision includes, when you need it and what it saves.',
      summary: 'A construction supervisor is responsible for professional control of the works on behalf of the developer or owner: checking that construction matches the plans, specification, permit and standards, approving the contractor\'s invoices by actual progress, tracking schedule and budget, and documenting defects and demanding their correction. Good supervision is the difference between a project finished on time and budget and years of repairs and disputes.',
      sections: [
        { h2: 'The supervisor\'s duties through the project', steps: ['**Before construction** — reviewing plans, specification and bill of quantities, helping select a contractor and draft the contract, and scheduling.', '**During construction** — regular site visits, inspecting works before they are covered (foundations, rebar, waterproofing, piping), lab tests (concrete), quality control and permit compliance.', '**Money** — checking and approving contractor invoices by actual progress, controlling changes and extras, tracking the budget.', '**Schedule** — tracking milestones, spotting delays, early warning.', '**Documentation** — site diary, periodic reports, photos, defect lists.', '**Handover** — handover inspections, defect protocol, repair tracking and the defects period.'] },
        { h2: 'What is actually inspected on site', bullets: ['Match to dimensions and plans (setbacks, levels, openings).', 'Material quality against the specification — no unapproved "equivalents".', 'Works covered later: waterproofing, rebar, piping, insulation.', 'Site safety and compliance with authorities.', 'Coordination between subcontractors.'] },
        { h2: 'Supervision vs. project management', paras: ['Supervision focuses on controlling construction on site. Project management is broader — coordinating everything from design and permits, through tenders and contracts, to handover, including the overall budget and schedule. In small projects one person does both; in large ones they are separated. [Project management and supervision](/en/services/project-management-supervision/) — how it works with us.'] },
        { h2: 'When you need a supervisor', bullets: ['In every new construction project — supervision is professional routine.', 'Building a private house — when the owner is not a professional, the supervisor is their eyes.', 'In a combination deal — a supervisor on the landowner\'s behalf protects their interest opposite the developer and contractor.', 'In a major renovation or addition.'] },
      ],
      faq: [
        { q: 'What does a construction supervisor do?', a: 'On behalf of the developer or owner, checks that on-site construction matches the plans, specification, permit and standards; approves contractor invoices by actual progress; tracks schedule and budget; and documents defects and demands correction.' },
        { q: 'What is the difference between a supervisor and a project manager?', a: 'The supervisor focuses on controlling construction on site. The project manager coordinates the whole project — design, permits, tenders, contracts, contractors, budget and schedule — from start to handover.' },
        { q: 'Is construction supervision mandatory?', a: 'The law requires a party responsible for inspecting execution under the permit (usually an engineer/architect), but close supervision on the client\'s behalf is not a legal duty — it is a professional decision that almost always pays for itself in fewer mistakes, extras and disputes.' },
      ],
